# A set literal is a UNION of three parts: the literal integers in i64, the
# literal non-integers in items, and the interval COMPONENTS in comps.  A set
# like `1, 2, Z[7~9]` puts two elements in the first part and the other three
# in the last, and vm_set_to_array used to refuse any set with a component:
#
#     if (s->kind != 0 || s->compCount > 0) return -1;
#
# so list() and len() reported the set as empty or nil -- while `in`, `min` and
# `max`, which walk the components, were correct all along.  The same refusal
# hid the case where ONE element of the literal is written on its own, which the
# compiler still wraps in a set: `z = Z[1~3]` is a NEW_SET over one component,
# so `len(z)` was 0 even though `len(Z[1~3])` was 3.
#
# A component is only enumerable when it has two finite endpoints and a lattice.
# `Z` on its own, `R`, and an interval with an open-into-infinity end are not,
# and must stay refused rather than be reported as empty.
#
# See docs/AUDIT.md §1.10.
b = 1, 2, Z[7~9]
say list(b)
c = Z[1~3], Z[5~7]
say list(c)
d = 1, Z[1~3]
say list(d)
e = Z[1~3]
say list(e)
f = Z, 1
say list(f)
p = 1, 2, 3
q = p, 4
say list(q)
say "setcomp-ok union=" + str(len(b)) + "/" + str(list(b)[0]) + "," + str(list(b)[4]) + " two=" + str(len(c)) + "/" + str(list(c)[3]) + " overlap=" + str(len(d)) + "/" + str(list(d)[2]) + " single=" + str(len(e)) + " nested=" + str(len(q)) + "/" + str(list(q)[3]) + " size=" + str(count(Z[1~3])) + " infinite=" + str(len(f)) + "," + str(list(f))
